{"causes":["The rise of the Dominican Order in Europe during the 13th century provided William of Paris with a unique platform for intellectual inquiry and theological debate.","The intellectual climate of the Late Middle Ages, characterized by the interplay between faith and reason, fostered an environment in which William of Paris could explore complex theological questions.","King Louis IX's reign marked a period of significant political and cultural upheaval in France, during which William of Paris was able to apply his theological expertise to inform the monarch's policies."],"effects":["William of Paris's work on human psychology contributed significantly to the development of Western philosophy, influencing thinkers such as Thomas Hobbes and René Descartes.","His involvement in the Fourth Lateran Council helped shape the theological understanding of the Catholic Church, particularly with regards to the nature of humanity.","The Dominican Order's emphasis on intellectual inquiry and theological debate was, in part, a response to William of Paris's pioneering work on human psychology."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"thomas-aquinas","sourceName":"Thomas Aquinas","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"william-of-paris","targetName":"William of Paris","context":"As a prominent figure within the Dominican Order, William of Paris was deeply influenced by Thomas Aquinas's philosophical and theological ideas."},{"sourceSlug":"fourth-lateran-council","sourceName":"Fourth Lateran Council","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"william-of-paris","targetName":"William of Paris","context":"The Fourth Lateran Council, which took place in 1215, provided a significant platform for William of Paris to apply his theological expertise."},{"sourceSlug":"king-louis-ix","sourceName":"King Louis IX","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"william-of-paris","targetName":"William of Paris","context":"As an advisor to King Louis IX, William of Paris was able to apply his theological expertise to inform the monarch's policies."},{"sourceSlug":"paris-france","sourceName":"Paris, France","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"william-of-paris","targetName":"William of Paris","context":"Born around 1220 in Paris, France, William of Paris spent his early years in this city before embarking on a career as a theologian and advisor to the royal court."},{"sourceSlug":"dominican-order","sourceName":"Dominican Order","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"william-of-paris","targetName":"William of Paris","context":"The Dominican Order, to which William of Paris belonged, placed a strong emphasis on intellectual inquiry and theological debate, providing him with a unique platform for his work."},{"sourceSlug":"late-middle-ages","sourceName":"Late Middle Ages","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"william-of-paris","targetName":"William of Paris","context":"The intellectual climate of the Late Middle Ages, characterized by the interplay between faith and reason, fostered an environment in which William of Paris could explore complex theological questions."}],"places":[{"name":"Paris, France","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Fourth Lateran Council","role":"Location"},{"name":"King's Court","role":"Institution"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: William of Paris","type":"Reference article","slug":"william-of-paris"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q3119907","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_of_Paris_(inquisitor)"],"tags":["Religious Figures","comparative-religion","doctrine-development","cultural-diffusion"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"William of Paris (died 1314), also known as Guillaume de Paris, was a French Dominican friar, theologian, and Inquisitor who served as the confessor and adviser of King Philip IV of France ('Philip the Fair') and who played a central role in one of the most dramatic and controversial episodes of medieval history: the suppression of the Order of the Knights Templar (1307–1312). As the royal confessor with royal authority to act as inquisitor, William of Paris conducted the initial interrogations of the arrested Templars following their sudden arrest on Friday 13 October 1307 — an event that has resonated in Western culture ever since, including as the claimed origin of the superstition around Friday the 13th.

William of Paris's role in the Templar affair was to extract confessions from the arrested Knights under torture — the standard inquisitorial procedure of the period — obtaining admissions of heresy (denial of Christ, obscene rituals, sodomy) that Philip IV used to justify the mass arrest and that formed the basis of the subsequent trials. Pope Clement V (the Avignon pope, himself under French political pressure) initially suspended the inquisition but eventually authorised a formal Church trial at the Council of Vienne (1311–1312), at which the Order was dissolved. The grand master Jacques de Molay and three other Templar leaders were burned at the stake in Paris in 1314.

Beyond the Templar affair, William of Paris was a significant figure in late medieval Dominican intellectual and institutional life — a theologian who participated in the vigorous scholastic debates of the early 14th century and who represented the fusion of royal confessor, inquisitor, and Dominican friar that characterised the relationship between the Capetian monarchy and the mendicant orders. His career illustrates the intimate connection between French royal power, the Dominican inquisition, and the Avignon papacy in the early 14th century.","subjects":["Medieval France","Dominican Order","Classical Era","Inquisition","Knights Templar","Medieval Church","Medieval History","Capetian France"],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"William of Paris's work on human psychology had a lasting impact on Western philosophy, influencing prominent thinkers for centuries to come.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
